# Environment template. Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored and must never be committed. This file is the only
# env file in version control, and it holds placeholders only.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — Neon Postgres
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two URLs, and they are not interchangeable.
#
# DATABASE_URL is the POOLED connection: the host contains `-pooler`. Neon
# fronts it with PgBouncer, which is what makes it safe for serverless, where
# every cold start would otherwise open its own Postgres connection and exhaust
# the project's limit. Every runtime query in the app uses this one.
#
# DIRECT_URL is the same database WITHOUT `-pooler`. Prisma Migrate needs it:
# the migration engine issues session-level statements (advisory locks, schema
# introspection) that PgBouncer's transaction pooling cannot carry. It is used
# by `prisma migrate` and `prisma db push` only — never at runtime.
#
# Both are on the Neon dashboard under Connection Details; toggle "Connection
# pooling" to switch between them.
#
# `sslmode=require` is mandatory — Neon refuses unencrypted connections.
# `pgbouncer=true` tells Prisma to disable prepared statements, which
# transaction-mode pooling cannot support.
# `connection_limit=1` keeps each serverless invocation to a single connection.

DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-example-123456-pooler.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require&pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-example-123456.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin gate
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Single-user auth for the future /admin routes. There is no user table and no
# registration flow — one password, one session cookie.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD is the login password. Anything with real entropy; it is the
# only thing between the public internet and the content editor.
#
# ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET signs the session cookie so it cannot be forged. It must
# be at least 32 characters and must NOT be the same value as ADMIN_PASSWORD.
# Generate one with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# Rotating this secret invalidates every existing session, which is the
# intended way to force a logout everywhere.

ADMIN_PASSWORD="choose-a-strong-password"
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET="run-the-command-above-and-paste-the-64-hex-characters-here"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudinary — image uploads from the admin panel
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All three come from the Cloudinary dashboard, under Settings -> API Keys.
#
# CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME is not a secret — it is part of every delivery URL.
# CLOUDINARY_API_KEY is semi-public: it travels with each upload request.
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET is the real credential and must never leave the server.
#
# None of these are prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, which is the point. The browser gets
# a signature from `/api/admin/upload-signature` — an authenticated route — and
# posts the file straight to Cloudinary with it. The secret only ever exists in
# `src/lib/cloudinary.ts`, and the signature it produces is scoped to one folder
# and expires within the hour. An unsigned upload preset would have been less
# code and would have shipped a permanent write token in the client bundle.
#
# With these unset, every other part of the admin still works — the upload
# widgets replace themselves with a note saying what is missing, and existing
# image URLs keep rendering.
#
# Uploads land under `portfolio/projects`, `portfolio/certifications` and
# `portfolio/profile`, which are fixed in `src/lib/cloudinary.ts` so a caller
# cannot aim one somewhere else.

CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME="your-cloud-name"
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY="123456789012345"
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET="paste-the-api-secret-here"
